WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.305 --> 00:00:03.555
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:07.710
- I am Neil Thompson.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.570
I'm the Director of
the Future Tech Project

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.510
here at the Computer
Science and AI Lab at MIT.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.460
And we have some questions
about Moore's Law and AI

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.160
that I'm gonna be answering today.

00:00:18.307 --> 00:00:20.550
"As we approach physical limitations

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:23.250
of silicon-based chip technology,

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.957
is Moore's Law still relevant?"

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.780
So I think this is a
really important question

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:29.880
because for decades and decades,

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:32.310
computing has been shaped by Moore's Law,

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.580
this pattern where as we
miniaturized our transistors,

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:37.650
these fundamental pieces of the chip.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:39.090
As we miniaturized them,

00:00:39.090 --> 00:00:41.250
that allowed us to put more
and more transistors on them.

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:43.470
And that made our computers
much more powerful

00:00:43.470 --> 00:00:45.210
and they used those smaller transistors,

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:47.880
used less energy so we
could run chips faster.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.980
And this just meant that
there were all kinds of things

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.063
that we could do today

00:00:51.063 --> 00:00:53.550
that we just could not do in the past.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.750
As you shrink these things,

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:57.420
at some point you get
down to the atomic level,

00:00:57.420 --> 00:00:58.860
and you get to this point where you say,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.980
well, now all of a sudden
we have quantum effects

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.150
and other things that are gonna start

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.340
interfering with the way
our transistors work.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.810
And that's gonna be a real challenge

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:09.690
to making them ever smaller
and making them ever better.

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:12.510
And in fact, back in 2004 and 2005,

00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.270
we already lost a big part of that gain

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:17.970
when we stopped our ability
to speed up the processors

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.230
that we were using.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:21.480
Now we're still getting the
last vestiges of Moore's Law.

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.850
We can still miniaturize
these transistors more.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.320
And so we're still
getting more transistors,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.850
we just can't run them as fast.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:29.400
And people may be aware that
we're having these problems now

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:30.660
where sometimes you have to like

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:31.800
turn off parts of your chips

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.360
sometimes called dark silicon

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:36.210
in order to not use all
the power at the same time

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:39.000
and produce too much that
would heat up the chip.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.390
So then the question is like,
is Moore's Law still relevant?

00:01:42.390 --> 00:01:44.520
And the answer is, it's
relevant for some people

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:46.080
but not for others.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:48.720
Already, those people
where the speed increase

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.070
was the important thing,

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:51.870
they've already lost a lot of the benefit.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:54.210
And that's actually,
unfortunately many of us, right?

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:56.730
If you're running a lot of
programs on your computer,

00:01:56.730 --> 00:01:57.570
a lot of the things you do,

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:00.450
you care mostly about how
fast you can run them.

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:02.310
And we've already lost
a lot of that benefit.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.660
There are however, still some people

00:02:03.660 --> 00:02:05.520
who are doing really advanced stuff,

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:06.900
like some of the stuff in AI

00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:08.910
and those AI chips are really using

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.260
lots and lots of transistors

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:12.600
and so they're still
taking advantage of it.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:14.040
So that'll still go on for a little while.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.870
Our cell phones are another example,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.300
but we already see this narrowing

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.250
of the people who are
benefiting from Moore's Law

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:23.587
and we don't have that much
longer until it's gonna run out.

00:02:23.587 --> 00:02:25.470
"What new materials like graphene

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.290
or technologies like 3D
stacking in chip design

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.510
might contribute to the
trend of Moore's Law

00:02:30.510 --> 00:02:32.490
beyond the limitations of silicon?

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:35.400
Unfortunately, the answer
here is that it's pretty hard

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:38.280
to know which of these
technologies are gonna win.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.740
And there are a couple of
reasons why that's true.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.030
So there are some technologies

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:44.910
that are clear are gonna be
useful in the short term.

00:02:44.910 --> 00:02:47.880
So 3D stacking is this
idea that previously

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:50.130
mostly our chips were
sort of like a plane,

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:51.900
so it was just like, and it was,

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:53.790
now we might be more like building it up.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:55.470
So you can imagine sort
of building high rises

00:02:55.470 --> 00:02:58.470
rather than having low rise
houses or things like that.

00:02:58.470 --> 00:02:59.880
And as we build them up,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:01.590
we get more and more transistors.

00:03:01.590 --> 00:03:03.090
That will definitely
give us some advantages

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:05.760
and we're already using
that in computer memory.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:08.241
The problem is that when
we run these transistors

00:03:08.241 --> 00:03:10.470
in our processors, they produce heat.

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:13.800
And that's been the limitation
for decades and decades.

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.890
That production of heat, limits
how fast we can run them.

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:17.850
And the problem is of course,

00:03:17.850 --> 00:03:20.100
that if you start stacking these
things on top of each other

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:21.630
and they're all producing heat,

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:24.240
now it's harder to get that heat away.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.540
And so I think 3D stacking
will be useful in some places,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.950
but not that much in others.

00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:32.460
Another short term technology
that may matter here as well,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.500
is this idea of what
sometimes called chiplets.

00:03:34.500 --> 00:03:36.540
And it's this idea that
rather than designing

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.660
each of our chips brand
new, we might build chips

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:41.730
and sort of stick them
together like legos.

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:43.290
And again, that has the possibility

00:03:43.290 --> 00:03:44.850
for some small gains in the short run

00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:46.860
because it allows us to be
a little bit more modular

00:03:46.860 --> 00:03:48.930
and save some costs,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.300
but it doesn't give us the
same kind of scale of benefits

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:53.520
that we had from Moore's Law.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:56.070
Now in the longer term
we have other things like

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.960
spintronics and graphene,

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.573
and other things that
people may have heard of,

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.380
those things, some of them
have a lot of promise,

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.030
but it's really very untested.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:08.280
These are all early stage technologies.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:09.300
And one of the things

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:11.190
that I've been talking
to policymakers about

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:12.690
that I think is really important

00:04:12.690 --> 00:04:15.240
is that we're just
investing far too little

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:18.240
in generating these next
generation technologies.

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.160
So I would say that we probably need

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.220
to be investing maybe 10 or
a 100 times as much money

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.010
as the United States as we are right now.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.980
So this is a real shortfall
and if we don't invest,

00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:30.750
we're gonna have this
longer and longer gap

00:04:30.750 --> 00:04:32.940
until these next technologies get here.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.290
And that's gonna mean that the benefits

00:04:34.290 --> 00:04:35.760
that we get for computing,

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.770
we're just not gonna get
them for much longer.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:39.420
And so that's a real deficit

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:41.580
that I really think the
government should try and address.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.860
And I hope that we can get the word out

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:46.103
that this is a really
important thing to talk about.

00:04:47.257 --> 00:04:50.190
"In the context of slowing
hardware advancements,

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:53.010
what are the key algorithmic
innovations in AI

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:54.837
that continue to drive its success?"

00:04:55.770 --> 00:04:58.800
So this is a really interesting
and important question

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.160
because if Moore's Law is
slowing, as I've told you,

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:03.720
why is it that we're seeing this

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:06.630
incredible explosion in
AI and how good it is?

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:08.340
And the answer is a couple of things

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:10.320
are going on at the same time.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:12.450
So one is that AI is better able

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:14.010
to take advantage of computer hardware

00:05:14.010 --> 00:05:16.140
than most types of things that we run.

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:19.200
That's just because it can do
more calculations in parallel.

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.240
And parallel is something
computers do really well

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:22.920
and AI uses a lot of.

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:24.150
Most of the things we run

00:05:24.150 --> 00:05:25.710
don't have that much parallelism

00:05:25.710 --> 00:05:28.350
and so it's hard to get
that full advantage of it.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:33.000
Another is just that AI as
an area actually is making

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:35.760
very rapid progress in algorithms as well.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.180
And so that's a big advantage
that's coming in too.

00:05:39.180 --> 00:05:41.250
But the biggest thing
that's driving AI right now

00:05:41.250 --> 00:05:43.767
is actually we're just
spending more and more money

00:05:43.767 --> 00:05:45.600
to do these models.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:46.800
And so it used to be,

00:05:46.800 --> 00:05:50.280
if you look at a cutting
edge model from the 1970s,

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:51.420
what people are running it on

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.430
is just a couple of processors, right?

00:05:53.430 --> 00:05:55.680
So think about buying a computer,

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:56.640
and you might have a computer

00:05:56.640 --> 00:05:57.780
or you might have a few computers,

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:01.050
but it was running on a relatively
small amount of hardware.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:04.560
Today when people train GPT-4 for example,

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:06.990
they're running on tens
of thousands of chips

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:10.140
at the same time and they're
not running them for hours,

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.230
they're running them for
weeks or even months.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.630
And so there's just a huge amount,

00:06:15.630 --> 00:06:17.100
more resources we're putting in.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:20.340
And that's a big, big part
of why AI is accelerating

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:22.170
even in the face of Moore's Law,

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:24.330
not giving us the efficiency improvements

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:25.380
that we used to have.

00:06:27.217 --> 00:06:29.460
"How sustainable is
Moore's Law progression

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.280
in light of rising costs
and environmental concerns?

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:34.021
Is there a limit to its economic

00:06:34.021 --> 00:06:36.300
or environmental viability?"

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:40.650
It's hard to overstate how
important Moore's Law has been

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.120
for the economic and environmental

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:43.623
sustainability of computing.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:46.170
If you go back and talk
to some of the people

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:48.300
who were at the very
early days of computing,

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:50.287
they're often saying things like,

00:06:50.287 --> 00:06:54.233
"We knew that you could
have video conferencing,

00:06:54.233 --> 00:06:57.390
we knew that you could do these
really complicated things.

00:06:57.390 --> 00:06:59.640
We just knew we didn't have
enough computing power."

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:02.100
And it was just totally
unreasonable to say

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:05.130
we're gonna spend so much money
or devote so many resources

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.120
to do these things.

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:07.410
And so they just didn't do them.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:10.590
And it took decades of
computers getting better

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:11.940
for those things to get into reach

00:07:11.940 --> 00:07:14.910
and for us to say, "Okay, now
that's reasonably affordable."

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.470
Now that's an amount of resources

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.080
we could actually imagine
spending on this problem.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.030
And the challenge we have today

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:22.950
is that as Moore's loss slows down,

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.170
we've lost that efficiency gain, right?

00:07:25.170 --> 00:07:26.685
Getting twice as many transistors

00:07:26.685 --> 00:07:28.470
for the same amount of dollars

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:31.353
or for the same amount of
resources was a huge benefit.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:35.436
And so for decades and
decades we saw Moore's Law

00:07:35.436 --> 00:07:39.090
was sort of driving the
improvement that we had.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:42.450
But as I just told you,
in AI that's not the case.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:44.030
The way we're doing in AI

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:45.780
is we are actually scaling that up.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:47.880
And that means there's a big environmental

00:07:47.880 --> 00:07:50.730
and economic cost that is
coming into these models.

00:07:50.730 --> 00:07:53.520
And people have estimated
that GPT-4, for example,

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.710
cost a hundred million dollars to train

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:59.520
and people are preparing to
spend 10 or a 100 times as much

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.530
in some of the coming
models that are going.

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.780
So these are enormously expensive systems

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:04.800
that they're building.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.180
And we should start to worry

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:08.580
because those big systems
with all of those resources

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:10.770
also produce more greenhouse gases

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:13.320
and that becomes less and
less sustainable for us.

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:15.577
And so I think one of the
things we need to do is say,

00:08:15.577 --> 00:08:17.550
"Well, if we're not
getting that improvement

00:08:17.550 --> 00:08:20.010
from Moore's Law and we are
skilling these things up

00:08:20.010 --> 00:08:21.480
in this way, what are the other things

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:24.210
that we can do in order
to make these chips

00:08:24.210 --> 00:08:26.940
more environmentally and
economically efficient?"

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:28.050
And people are working on that.

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:29.910
There's a lot of really
interesting algorithm work

00:08:29.910 --> 00:08:31.410
going on in these areas,

00:08:31.410 --> 00:08:33.030
but it's a real challenge
in order to meet,

00:08:33.030 --> 00:08:35.340
because right now we're
scaling up even faster

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.690
than we're able to do that.

00:08:38.107 --> 00:08:39.300
"With the end of Moore's Law,

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:41.100
are we approaching a fundamental shift

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:43.300
in the economic model
of the tech industry?"

00:08:44.280 --> 00:08:45.510
I think not only are we moving

00:08:45.510 --> 00:08:47.100
to a different economic model,

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:49.620
but we're actually moving
back to an economic model

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:52.500
that looks a lot more like the
things in our regular lives

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:53.520
and away from a model

00:08:53.520 --> 00:08:56.490
that was really just very
different and very unusual.

00:08:56.490 --> 00:09:00.570
So if I told you, "Oh,
imagine we have a motor."

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:02.220
You would say, "Okay,
I can imagine a motor,

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:05.520
but the motors that I picture
look awfully different."

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:09.596
Right, the motor that's on a
plane is huge and powerful.

00:09:09.596 --> 00:09:13.920
The one that is in a blender
is sort of moderately powerful.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.450
And the one that is in like a fan

00:09:15.450 --> 00:09:18.720
that is blowing on you is
not very powerful at all.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.534
There's enormous
differences in the materials

00:09:21.534 --> 00:09:24.060
and the design choices that are made

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:26.280
for those different types of motors.

00:09:26.280 --> 00:09:27.960
Historically for computer chips,

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:31.200
we have not had this
kind of specialization.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.346
Computer chips built these CPUs,

00:09:33.346 --> 00:09:35.100
these central processing units,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.440
and they've been designed
to do everything.

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:39.420
In fact, if you talk to Intel
architects, they'll tell you

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:41.760
that you sort of, these
chips are a compromise

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:43.800
so that they do everything well.

00:09:43.800 --> 00:09:45.750
And we've had this remarkable durability

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:48.570
of not specializing, of having
just a single technology

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:51.064
that we use for all
kinds of things, right?

00:09:51.064 --> 00:09:53.280
And you know, in the rest of
the world that would seem odd.

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:55.410
Imagine a motor that you put in your plane

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:56.670
when you go in an airplane

00:09:56.670 --> 00:09:58.620
and then you put on your
blender when you get home,

00:09:58.620 --> 00:10:00.420
like that's a very different world.

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:03.000
And so the reason that
we were able to have that

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:04.860
for so long is precisely

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:08.340
because computers were
getting better so fast

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:09.960
that it wasn't worth specializing it.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.760
'Cause you'd say, "Well,
I would get some benefit

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.970
of specializing it, but then
I'm gonna get that benefit

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.430
from Moore's Law in a year or two anyway.

00:10:17.430 --> 00:10:18.660
And so I'd rather just wait

00:10:18.660 --> 00:10:20.190
and use this generic technology

00:10:20.190 --> 00:10:22.590
that we can all take advantage of."

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:23.970
Okay, so the question asked about this

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:25.830
economic shift, what's happening?

00:10:25.830 --> 00:10:28.260
Well, as Moore's Law slows down,

00:10:28.260 --> 00:10:29.640
now we need to start specializing

00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:31.620
and we're gonna start
specializing in computer chips

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:32.970
in just the way that we do

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:35.040
in almost every other
product in our lives.

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:37.230
And that means we're gonna have chips

00:10:37.230 --> 00:10:39.210
that are specifically
designed for our phones.

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:41.460
We already have those.

00:10:41.460 --> 00:10:43.050
Chips that are designed for AI.

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:44.730
Okay, we already have those as well,

00:10:44.730 --> 00:10:47.880
but we're also gonna get all
kinds of other chips coming in.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:49.860
And so this is a real departure for it.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.570
And you know, there are horror stories

00:10:51.570 --> 00:10:53.130
of people in the '70s and '80s

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.220
who thought specialization
is the thing of that moment.

00:10:56.220 --> 00:10:58.470
And it wasn't because of Moore's Law.

00:10:58.470 --> 00:11:01.140
Now all of those people are
finally getting their moment

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:03.300
and all the specialization is happening.

00:11:03.300 --> 00:11:04.950
The great part about that is,

00:11:04.950 --> 00:11:06.510
like as we do that specialization,

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.340
there are some gains
that we can get from it.

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:10.170
The hard part about it is that

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.420
we're gonna get this fragmentation, right?

00:11:12.420 --> 00:11:15.240
We're no longer gonna have
a single operating system

00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:16.290
that works on these things.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:19.380
A single programming
language that used in,

00:11:19.380 --> 00:11:21.330
you're gonna get all kinds of differences

00:11:21.330 --> 00:11:23.010
and things are gonna become
much more specialized.

00:11:23.010 --> 00:11:25.140
And that's gonna be a lot more
complicated for the industry

00:11:25.140 --> 00:11:26.493
and for all of us as well.

00:11:27.667 --> 00:11:29.550
"As we approach the limits of Moore's Law,

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:32.040
how do you see the role
of software optimizations

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:34.830
in advancing computing capabilities?"

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:38.000
So this same benefit that
we were just talking about

00:11:38.000 --> 00:11:40.110
of Moore's Law, meaning that

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:42.000
everyone stuck with the same chip,

00:11:42.000 --> 00:11:44.070
those gains were also really used

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:46.050
by software designers in the past.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:48.120
And in particular they were
used to design software

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:49.830
that was very modular.

00:11:49.830 --> 00:11:52.830
So imagine this, you might
have like a particular

00:11:52.830 --> 00:11:54.870
programming language or
a particular set of code

00:11:54.870 --> 00:11:56.970
that is for example, talking
to the hardware, right?

00:11:56.970 --> 00:11:58.470
It might be the operating system

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.600
or is talking to some external device

00:12:00.600 --> 00:12:01.740
or something like that.

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:04.170
You would design that and you would say,

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:05.760
if another program wants to talk to it,

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:06.593
if it wants to say,

00:12:06.593 --> 00:12:09.180
"Hey, I wanna use the
microphone on your phone",

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:10.353
or something like that,

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:13.837
it calls that other program and says,

00:12:13.837 --> 00:12:15.960
"Do this for me and send
me back the answer."

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:18.660
And there's this very nice clean interface

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:21.210
that says you only have to
tell me these few details

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:23.640
and I'll take care of everything
else behind the scenes.

00:12:23.640 --> 00:12:25.890
The problem is that when you do this,

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.320
you often pay a cost
in terms of efficiency

00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:30.738
that it's often maybe
like in your program,

00:12:30.738 --> 00:12:32.940
you're doing analysis at one level

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:34.260
and then you have to convert,

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:35.520
maybe you have to convert the data

00:12:35.520 --> 00:12:38.070
or you have to convert the
way that you're handling stuff

00:12:38.070 --> 00:12:39.780
into another form so that you can use

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:41.970
this interface that you have.

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:43.410
So that's very inefficient,

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:44.970
but it can be very worthwhile

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:46.650
if it makes programming easier.

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.690
As long as Moore's Law
is giving us this benefit

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:51.540
year after year of making
it cheaper and cheaper

00:12:51.540 --> 00:12:53.100
to run our chips.

00:12:53.100 --> 00:12:55.380
As Moore's Law ends, we lose that gain.

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:58.080
And so now if you wanna make
your programs more efficient,

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:01.530
you're gonna want to take
back that modularization,

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:03.660
that inefficiency that you
were willing to build in

00:13:03.660 --> 00:13:05.790
to make programming these things easier.

00:13:05.790 --> 00:13:06.750
You're gonna take that back

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.060
in order to make running
them more efficient.

00:13:09.060 --> 00:13:10.380
And so I think that's a big thing

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:11.460
that people are gonna be doing.

00:13:11.460 --> 00:13:13.808
And in some of the work that
we've published in science,

00:13:13.808 --> 00:13:15.900
we've shown that in some particular cases

00:13:15.900 --> 00:13:17.850
there can be enormous gains

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.500
from taking back that efficiency.

00:13:21.697 --> 00:13:24.390
"How will the slowing or
potential end of Moore's Law

00:13:24.390 --> 00:13:27.450
impact the development of
AI and big data analytics,

00:13:27.450 --> 00:13:28.590
which are heavily dependent

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:30.360
on increasing computational power?"

00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:33.960
So much of what we're doing
right now to scale up AI

00:13:33.960 --> 00:13:36.540
is actually just spending a lot more money

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:39.780
to build more chips and
run more chips for longer

00:13:39.780 --> 00:13:41.820
than in the short run,
the change in Moore's Law

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:43.110
is not gonna have much of an effect

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:45.570
because we're scaling up so rapidly.

00:13:45.570 --> 00:13:47.850
But as these models get
more and more expensive,

00:13:47.850 --> 00:13:50.850
and again, GPT4, it's costing about

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:52.320
a hundred million dollars to train

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.030
and people are expecting
the next generations

00:13:54.030 --> 00:13:56.880
to be 10 or a 100 times
more expensive, right?

00:13:56.880 --> 00:13:58.770
We are indefinitely gonna get to limits

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:00.060
where we say these models

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:02.580
are getting very, very expensive to build.

00:14:02.580 --> 00:14:04.800
And then the lack of Moore's
Law is really gonna hurt us

00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:08.040
because what you hope and what
we have traditionally had,

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:09.630
is that even if you introduce something

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:13.350
that was very expensive over
time it got much, much cheaper.

00:14:13.350 --> 00:14:15.450
So for example, in the
early days of computing,

00:14:15.450 --> 00:14:19.830
NASA paid for a lot of computing
power on the spaceship.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:23.400
But today that amount of
computing is available

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:25.546
for much, much less
because of this progress

00:14:25.546 --> 00:14:27.243
that has come from Moore's Law.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:31.410
What we would hope is that that
would happen in AI as well,

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:34.110
and we will get some of it
because AI is also improving

00:14:34.110 --> 00:14:35.820
in terms of its software efficiency

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:37.260
and algorithmic efficiency.

00:14:37.260 --> 00:14:39.030
But not having the hardware part

00:14:39.030 --> 00:14:40.430
is gonna be a big challenge.

